# Visual Mood Lab — environment variables
#
# Copy this file to .env.local for local development. None of these are
# required to run locally — with everything unset, the app runs on PGlite
# (an embedded Postgres, no credentials needed) and stores uploads on disk
# under public/uploads/. Set these when you're ready to point at real
# infrastructure, typically when deploying to Vercel.

# --- Database --------------------------------------------------------
#
# A Postgres connection string, e.g. from Neon (neon.tech — the free tier
# is enough for this app). Unset: falls back to PGlite at ./.pglite/.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require

# --- Public URL ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# The address the site is served from. Used for the links in verification and
# password-reset emails, and as Next's `metadataBase` so link previews (the
# Open Graph image) point at the real site instead of localhost.
#
# On Vercel you can leave this UNSET: the app falls back to Vercel's own
# production / preview URL. Set it to override — e.g. a custom domain, or when
# emails must link to a specific address. Scheme + host (+ port); a path or
# trailing slash is ignored. See lib/site.ts.
#
# APP_URL=https://your-app.example

# --- Blob storage ------------------------------------------------------
#
# A Vercel Blob read/write token. Create a Blob store in your Vercel
# project's Storage tab and copy the token it gives you. Unset: uploads
# and generated posters are written to public/uploads/ instead — fine for
# local development, but Vercel's filesystem is read-only in production,
# so this MUST be set before deploying anything that uploads media or
# generates posters.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# --- Seeding -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# POST /api/seed migrates the database and loads the starter library. It is
# POST-only (a GET could be triggered by a link prefetch or another site).
#
# Development: no configuration needed —
#     curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/seed
#   (add ?fresh=1 to drop the tables first — dev only).
#
# Production: disabled unless BOTH of these are set, and every request must
# carry the secret. `fresh` is always refused in production.
#     curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $SEED_ADMIN_SECRET" \
#       https://your-app.example/api/seed
# Set them, redeploy, seed once, then remove ALLOW_SEED_ROUTE and redeploy.
# "0" / "false" / "off" count as OFF for ALLOW_SEED_ROUTE.
#
# Generate a secret:  openssl rand -base64 32     (24+ characters required)
#
# Surrounding whitespace in SEED_ADMIN_SECRET (a stray space or newline from a
# paste box) is ignored. If a request still gets "Unauthorized", the server log
# says why — lengths only, never a value: in Vercel open Logs and search "[seed]".
#
# ALLOW_SEED_ROUTE=
# SEED_ADMIN_SECRET=

# --- Rate limiting (Upstash Redis) --------------------------------------
#
# Login, signup, password-reset and resend-verification are limited per
# email AND per IP; upload signing and poster/snapshot captures are limited
# per account. Create a free Redis database at upstash.com and copy its REST
# URL and token. Unset: limits are SKIPPED (fine locally) — but in production
# that means no rate limiting at all, and the server log says so. The
# limiters fail OPEN: if Upstash is unreachable, requests are allowed and the
# error is logged once, rather than login breaking.
#
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
